From e42f188c27ac10a9f41d86331a3bcbde83f615ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-agent <agent@lccc.local>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:26:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] i686: make the CFG slot forwarder a fixpoint, and close
 three soundness holes

An external audit of the merged pass (PR #512) raised four findings. Three
are right and are fixed as prescribed; the fourth is fixed differently, on
measurement. Ten pins were written against the UNMODIFIED code and run
first, so every claim below rests on a test that failed before the fix and
passes after: six of them failed.

F1 -- loops. Available-value forwarding is a must analysis, and iterating it
upward from bottom cannot bootstrap a fact around a cycle. A slot stored
before a loop and left alone inside it satisfies OUT[head] = IN[head], so
IN[head] = meet(OUT[pre], OUT[head]) has two solutions -- empty, and the
fact -- and the upward iteration reaches only the empty one. The fact is
then missing not just inside the loop but everywhere downstream of it, which
is where the reload of a loop-carried value actually sits. The ready-gated
single sweep never even processed those blocks: head and tail waited on each
other and neither was ever requeued.

The iteration now seeds optimistically -- one reverse-postorder pass meeting
over NON-back predecessors only, which is exact for the acyclic part because
in RPO every non-back edge is already final -- then a worklist pass meets
over all predecessors and re-enqueues successors whenever a value moves.
Values only shrink after the seed and the lattice is finite, so it
converges. Soundness does not depend on which fixpoint it converges to: a
sound boundary (IN[0] pinned to "no register holds a value this function
stored" and never derived from OUT[0], because a back edge to the function's
own label makes block 0 its own predecessor), a sound transfer, and the
equations holding EXACTLY when the result is used together imply that every
fact in IN[b] holds on every real arrival at b, by induction on the length
of the executed prefix. The corollary is operational: a truncated iteration
is not a fixpoint, so exhausting the budget abandons the function without
applying anything.

Analysis and application are two sweeps over ONE shared transfer
(slot_fwd_block, apply: bool). Sharing is the point -- two copies of a
transfer this size drift, and a drift between what was proved and what was
rewritten is a miscompile rather than a missed optimisation.

The monotonicity argument offered for the two-sweep form does not hold as
merged: the implicit-write arm probed line_writes_reg_implicitly only for
registers the CURRENT state tracks, so a stronger IN stamped more registers
and produced a WEAKER OUT. That is anti-monotone, and chaotic iteration over
an anti-monotone transfer need not converge. The candidate set is now
source_regs -- the union of source registers over every StoreEbp in the
function. Function-level, therefore state-independent, and still every
register that can matter, because an entry can only be sourced from a
register some store in this function reads. Memoised per line, so the oracle
still runs once per line rather than once per line per iteration.

F2 -- computed branches. JmpIndirect was given no successors, so a block
reachable both through a jump table and by a direct edge -- a switch case a
goto or a loop back edge also enters -- met only its direct predecessor, and
the dispatch path contributed nothing. This is live today rather than
theoretical once loops work: the pin fails on the unmodified code.
Under-approximating a CFG is not the conservative direction for a must
analysis; it removes constraints, and removed constraints are what make a
meet too optimistic. Successors are now every block in the function, because
the table lives in .rodata and a text scan cannot read it. Two pins hold the
pair: a redefinition on any path still blocks the forward, and switch
forwarding is not simply switched off to achieve that.

F3 -- string ops. rep movsb / rep stosb write memory through %edi with no
displacement a text scan can see, so has_indirect_mem stayed false and the
pass forwarded straight across them. The corpus has 14 sites in 7 TUs where
a reload was deleted across a rep movsb. is_string_op is an ENUMERATED table
of the seven families by size, ORed into has_indirect at classification,
which also closes the same blind spot in the windowed pass. Enumerated
rather than derived because the obvious rule is wrong: a movs prefix plus a
trailing size strip matches movsbl, movsbw and movzbl, which are register
moves. In the transfer the family kills everything, escape or not -- a
bounded indirect store needs its address to EQUAL a tracked slot, while a
string op writes an unbounded range and needs only to OVERLAP one, so a
pointer merely near the frame reaches into it once the count is large
enough.

F4 -- indirect writes with no escape: fixed precisely, not blanket.
%esp-biased keys carry ESP_SLOT_BIAS = 1 << 24 exactly so they cannot be
confused with %ebp-relative ones, which means an %esp-biased slot is THIS
activation's frame -- locals and outgoing arguments -- not the caller's
region. The caller-owned range is positive-%ebp: the incoming-argument area,
which a va_list two frames up can name without anything in this function
ever forming a frame address. drop_caller_owned_slots kills exactly that
range and keeps the rest.

Measuring the alternative is what decides it. On the 795-TU corpus a blanket
kill_all gives 270,064 instructions and the precise rule 269,898: 166
instructions across 74 TUs turn on it, on the ordinary shape "update a
global through the PIC register while a frame temporary is live". And the
blanket rule does not buy the safety it was proposed for -- the pointer into
our outgoing-argument area that motivates it can be leaked by a CALLEE that
writes through it during a call, a site this arm does not govern and the
blanket kill does not touch either. The premise "no frame address was formed
here, so nothing outside can name our frame" is the one the Call arm already
runs on; applying it uniformly makes the two arms consistent, where a
stricter rule at one site and a laxer one at the other would only hide the
assumption. Both directions are pinned, the second on a two-path shape so
the meet and not just the kill distinguishes the rules.

Not touched: the windowed pass's point window, the frame extent model, the
phase-4-head placement and the narrow-store parameter. The one change
reaching the windowed pass is has_indirect gaining is_string_op, which closes
a blind spot rather than widening a window.

Evidence. Corpus instructions, pre-fix to post-fix: -1,755 at -O0, -393 at
-O1, -336 at -O2 and -O3, -354 at -Os, with the same 15 pre-existing compile
failures in both arms at every level. .text bytes at -O2: 901,504 to 900,217,
-1,287 (-0.143%), 107 TUs smaller and 14 larger, each larger one a refused
string-op forward verified by diff rather than assumed. All 14 changed
benchmark TUs improved. Paired two-binary runtime, arms alternating within
every repetition: geomean 0.9577 min / 0.9552 median, lz4_compress 0.9967
(N=31), nbody 0.9973, binary_trees 0.9866 at N=25 after a noisy +1.5% median
at N=15, nothing worse than 2% on min, every stdout and exit code identical.
2,622 unit tests pass (35 cfg_fwd); fmt and clippy clean; exec_diff over 667
runnable TUs gives 21 mismatches against the gcc oracle, the same 21 and the
same set as base, with the only two behaviour deltas adjudicated as a
non-deterministic benchmark and a test whose i386 expectation is wrong (both
reproduced by the kill-switch arm, so neither is this patch); 320 fuzz cases
over 16 generators, 0 divergences, 0 wrong against gcc, the three new
generators firing 20/20 each and accounting for 161 of the 221 instructions
removed. Compile time +2.1% min / +3.1% median, because the merged pass
skipped loop blocks and skipping them was the bug; acyclic functions pay
nothing extra, since the RPO seed pass is their fixpoint and the second pass
is seeded only with back-edge targets -- an optimisation verified
output-neutral on 795/795 TUs.
